  "id": "Compound::DB00563 - Disease::DOID:2531",
  "verbalization": "Methotrexate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Hematologic Cancer through multiple indirect pathways, primarily via its role as an enzyme inhibitor and its ability to induce gastrointestinal ulcers, cardiac tamponade, and esophageal cancer. These effects are mediated through shared biological mechanisms, such as the inhibition of enzymes involved in DNA synthesis and cell proliferation, which are critical in the development of hematologic malignancies. The presence of a common ancestor, the Enzyme Inhibitor, across these pathways underscores a potential shared molecular mechanism, suggesting that Methotrexate may exert its effects through a common biochemical pathway that influences cellular processes critical to hematologic cancer development. The integration of these pathways highlights Methotrexate's potential to contribute to the development of Hematologic Cancer through a broad range of physiological effects, emphasizing its role as a multi-targeted agent with diverse therapeutic implications.",
